{"slots": {"question": [{"claim": "The author investigates whether using stigmatizing labels like 'convicted criminal' in political advertisements against figures like Donald Trump causes negative spillover effects on how Democratic constituents perceive all individuals with criminal records.", "quotes": [{"text": "Leaning upon social categorization theory, propagating such labels on Trump may thereby spillover, impacting constituents’ perspectives of individuals with records more broadly.", "page": 1}]}], "central_claim": [{"claim": "Exposure to stigmatizing political labels like 'convicted criminal' reduces positive perceptions of individuals with criminal records, but providing a counter-narrative via perspective-getting can mitigate these negative effects.", "quotes": [{"text": "Results suggest that exposure to the Trump advertisement with the “convicted criminal” label had significant, negative impacts on Democrats’ perceptions of individuals with records, but improved perceived feelings of representation.", "page": 1}, {"text": "Findings suggest that the counter-stereotype vastly ameliorated the negative effects of the advertisement on Democrats’ perceptions of individuals with criminal records.", "page": 1}]}], "evidence": [{"claim": "The study used a survey experiment with 2,439 self-identified Democrats in the U.S. divided into a control group, a group exposed to a Trump advertisement with a stigmatizing label, and a group receiving a perspective-getting counter-narrative first.", "quotes": [{"text": "this preliminary study employs a survey experiment testing exposure to the Party’s advertisement, labeling Trump as a “convicted criminal” (n = 814) relative to no advertisement (n = 813), on Democrats’ perceptions of individuals with records and several dimensions of perceived representation.", "page": 1}, {"text": "the 2,439 participants were allocated approximately evenly across one of three different treatment groups: a) the Control group (n = 813); b) the Trump Ad Alone group (n = 814); and c) the Counter-Narrative First group (n = 812).", "page": 4}, {"text": "This survey was fielded amongst individuals residing within the United States who spoke fluent English and were members of the Democratic Party.", "page": 8}]}], "key_findings": [{"claim": "Exposure to the Trump advertisement containing the 'convicted criminal' label significantly decreases positive perceptions of individuals with criminal records.", "quotes": [{"text": "exposure to the Trump advertisement alone significantly decreased (positive) perceptions of individuals with criminal records", "page": 11}]}], "key_findings": [{"claim": "Providing a counter-narrative involving a system-impacted representative significantly increases positive perceptions of individuals with criminal records compared to the advertisement alone.", "quotes": [{"text": "exposure to the counter-narrative prior to being presented with the Trump advertisement increased positive perceptions of individuals with criminal records by more than 0.8 standard deviation (SD) units, compared to those presented with the Trump advertisement alone", "page": 11}]}], "key_findings": [{"claim": "A counter-narrative approach significantly reduces support for the use of stigmatizing political advertisements.", "quotes": [{"text": "exposure to the counter-narrative significantly decreased support for the Trump advertisement, relative to the Trump advertisement alone", "page": 11}]}], "key_findings": [{"claim": "The advertisement labeling Trump as a 'convicted criminal' increases perceptions that elected officials are motivated by self-interest rather than public service.", "quotes": [{"text": "exposure to the Trump advertisement alone... significantly increased participants beliefs that elected officials’ motivations were to serve the public, versus serving their own self-interest.", "page": 11}]}], "scope_and_limits": [{"claim": "The study is a preliminary work and requires field experiments to confirm and extend the findings.", "quotes": [{"text": "While well-powered and aptly-timed field experiments are needed to confirm and extend these findings, utilizing political messaging that elevates dedicated, justice-involved voices within the party rather than rely on stigmatizing attacks should be strongly considered and tested.", "page": 1}]}], "theoretical_positioning": [{"claim": "The paper utilizes social categorization theory to predict that stigmatizing labels will cause spillover effects on perceptions of group members.", "quotes": [{"text": "Leaning upon social categorization theory, propagating such labels on Trump may thereby spillover, impacting constituents’ perspectives of individuals with records more broadly.", "page": 1}]}], "interlocutor_claim": [{"claim": "The author disputes the idea that using an emotional, anger-inducing advertisement might be the most effective way to bolster political support, suggesting instead that policy alignment and perspective-getting are key to representation.", "quotes": [{"text": "One study found that anger-inducing political advertisements bolstered support and encouraged action specifically amongst members of the party producing the advertisement [33]. However, this is not necessarily concrete, in that alignment of policy standpoints has been shown to be crucial to feelings of representation [34–36].", "page": 3}]}]}}
